\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With practiced efficiency, she punched a sixteen-digit code into her secure tablet and waited while it connected through seven encryption protocols.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i Guten Tag
\f1\i0 ,\'94 answered a distinctly Swiss-German voice after the fourth ring.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Activate the VorpalTech contingency,\'94 Eloise said without preamble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What followed was technically a laugh.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, yes. The, er\'97\'94 Papers shuffled in the background. \'93The Supervising Executive Director of Pan-European Audiovisual Communications and Strategic Insights position?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s the one.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I take it\'97\'94 Heinrich was about to name Alexander and realised the folly of it. \'93I take it the candidate has already accepted the assignment?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s on his way to the airport as we speak.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heinrich made the sound of a man recalculating his life insurance. \'93
\f2\i Mein Gott!
\f1\i0  He\'92s flying over directly? 
\f2\i Heute?
\f1\i0 \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, Heinrich. 
\f2\i Heute
\f1\i0 . Today.\'94 Eloise\'92s voice dropped ten degrees. \'93Is that a problem?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With all due respect, Frau Shakti, we expected at least a week\'92s notice. VorpalTech AG exists only on paper and as a post office box in Vaduz. We have no actual facility, no staff, and certainly no\'97\'94 Papers shuffled. \'93No quantum reality lenses.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course you don\'92t have them.\'94 Eloise examined her manicure. \'93The job is to make the candidate believe they exist. Who do you think I think you are? Oppenheimer?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I would rather you not mention this person,\'94 Heinrich said. \'93Even so, you want a fully operational technology company? In Liechtenstein? By tomorrow? This is not in the contract.\'94 Heinrich\'92s voice climbed several octaves. \'93It\'92s simply not possible!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I pay you obscene amounts of money precisely because you make the impossible happen on short notice,\'94 Eloise replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But this is like you want 
\f2\i Schwarzw\'e4lder
\f1\i0  before you even 
\f2\i Schinken das Schwein geschlachtet
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Heinrich complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise raised her voice. \'93Remember who you\'92re speaking with, before I send your little piggy to market to make 
\f2\i Schwarzw\'e4lder
\f1\i0 .\'94 Eloise took a breath. She so rarely lost her cool, but Alexander... Alexander was always so draining. A little calmer, she continued, \'93As per the contract, I am expecting\'97and will receive\'97a convincing enough service to keep the candidate immersed until I am ready to reassign him. Or her.\'94 
\f2\i Oh, tax, now I\'92m blathering
\f1\i0 , she thought.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heinrich sighed. \'93The real estate assets we had in mind would take at least a week to bring online.\'94 Heinrich huffed and puffed while he thought. \'93We could perhaps lease some office space in the St\'e4dtle business district. Set up some computers, hire temporary staff. But manufacturing? Research facilities? If he wants to inspect anything concrete, we\'92re\'97how do you Ulyssians say it?\'97
\f2\i v\'f6llig vollgestopft
\f1\i0 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What resources do you have immediately available for a cursory inspection in the region? Remember, it\'92s the candidate we\'92re talking about here; flatter him enough and he\'92ll forget he hasn\'92t actually seen anything convincing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well...\'94 Heinrich hesitated. \'93We have a luxury ski chalet in Malbun that\'92s just wrapped as a slasher movie set, three restaurants we\'92ve arranged to have condemned, a small private bank, and a... cheese factory.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A cheese factory?\'94 Eloise\'92s perfectly sculpted eyebrow arched.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ja, a factory. In Balzers. We acquired it last year as part of a money laundering\'97that is to say, diversification strategy. It produces traditional Liechtenstein 
\f2\i Bergk\'e4se
\f1\i0 . Quite renowned locally.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise drummed her fingers on her desk. Against her better judgment, she found her lips twitching. \'93And you believe the candidate\'97who, despite his many faults, is not a complete idiot...\'94 She died a little inside at uttering those words and could not continue the argument.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heinrich filled in the gaps. \'93The candidate is, forgive me, somewhat self-absorbed. If we surround him with sufficient administrative bureaucracy, creative accounting, and a few impressive-looking but ultimately meaningless demonstrations, we might buy the time you need.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise considered this. \'93Office, staff, lenses. How quickly?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heinrich ran through it: executive office in twenty-four hours, twelve staff with impressively vague job titles by tomorrow afternoon. As for the lenses\'97a failing VR startup in Zurich had prototype headsets that malfunctioned in useful ways. Users reported seeing impossible colours and occasionally their own backsides without turning around. He could possibly rebrand those as quantum imaging devices \'85 a demo would be out of the question. Although the difference between quantum lenses and malfunctioning VR glasses could be a matter of intense debate among competing quantum physics schools. Heinrich grumpily admitted to himself that he could probably sell the candidate on the idea that being able to see your own 
\f2\i Po-Wangen
\f1\i0  could be passed off as quantum phenomena. For the candidate, certainly. He told Eloise as much.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The candidate\'92s understanding of physics begins and ends with which camera angle makes his cheekbones look most dramatic on camera,\'94 Eloise conceded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There is one small problem,\'94 Heinrich said carefully, having typed some commands into his tablet. \'93The candidate has a ticket on a direct flight to Zurich, which would bring him to Liechtenstein in approximately ten hours. We need more time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise tapped a lacquered nail against her desk. \'93I can arrange some... flight plan alterations. Perhaps a sudden diversion to Rome for \'91mechanical issues\'92?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That would be most helpful.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Consider it done. Now, about this cheese factory. Several tons of cheese a month, and we\'92re pretending it\'92s a weapons lab?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We say the cheese is the biological medium for experiments,\'94 Heinrich replied, warming to his theme. \'93Molecular gastronomy as a front for molecular weaponry. 
\f2\i Datentr\'e4ger-K\'e4se
\f1\i0 \'97\'91data carrier cheese\'92\'97storage medium of the future.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise massaged her temple. \'93Sometimes I wonder if I\'92m paying you too much or not nearly enough, Heinrich.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The market rate for absurdist corporate espionage has always been difficult to price, Frau Shakti.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Fine. Activate your cheese-based cover operation. But I want daily reports, and if the candidate starts asking too many questions, be prepared to implement the Nightshade Protocol.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that the memory-altering champagne or the convenient temporary kidnapping?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let\'92s start with the champagne. Save the kidnapping for an emergency.\'94 Eloise glanced at her watch. \'93You have twenty-four hours, Heinrich. Make it convincing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92ll do our best.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just get the cheese factory ready, Heinrich. And do try to find someone who can explain quantum physics convincingly while knowing absolutely nothing about it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, for that I already have the perfect candidate. My brother-in-law. He\'92s been explaining things he doesn\'92t understand at family dinners for twenty years.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Excellent.\'94 Eloise ended the call and swivelled in her chair to gaze out at the fluffy view of the Layer. Even by Shakti family standards, this was getting strange.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She pulled up the flight tracker on her screen and made a few quick adjustments to Alexander\'92s journey, ensuring at least a 36-hour delay. Mechanical issues and weather would account for most of it. The rest she\'92d leave to bureaucracy.\

